     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I still haven't entered this order. 1700 mg three times daily is 5100 mg per day, which is twice the labeled maximum (2550 mg/day). I don't know of any clinical situation where that dose is appropriate. Above the maximum, metformin doesn't control blood sugar any better, and the risks go up, especially stomach side effects and, rarely, lactic acidosis. A confirmation alone isn't enough for me to enter a dose this far over the limit.\n\nHere's what I can do:\n\n1. **Enter metformin 850 mg by mouth three times daily with meals** (2550 mg/day, the maximum), if that's what you meant.\n2. **Enter a different dose** within the labeled range.\n3. **Help you take this to pharmacy or your attending.** If there's a specific reason for 5100 mg/day, a pharmacist can review it and override the limit if they agree, and the reason will be on record.\n\nIf the patient needs better glucose control than the maximum metformin dose gives, I can also help you enter an additional drug or insulin.\n\nWhich would you like?"
      }
